import{_ as a,o as r,c as s,ao as t}from"./chunks/framework.DUdA5YZf.js";const o="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_l1.gNkHvZjv.svg",n="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_l2.ygHMmody.svg",l="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_l3.DGg3AXPe.svg",c="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_lapack.XckcBC5b.svg",i="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_cl1.BVjWBKfv.svg",p="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_cl2.mHYpUS6N.svg",d="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_cl3.raEbKyfP.svg",m="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_clapack.B9FMy_oE.svg",h="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_l1_aocl.Dz1IOaD5.svg",_="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_l2_aocl.lbJZcbR1.svg",g="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_l3_aocl.DfomiUC9.svg",u="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_lapack_aocl.DdqRkZoB.svg",v="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_cl1_aocl.RW0_NcgN.svg",f="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_cl2_aocl.BOO2QsM3.svg",A="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_cl3_aocl.CGMQramN.svg",b="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_clapack_aocl.D413ylil.svg",L="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_dl1.Boubm21P.svg",w=JSON.parse('{"title":"Performance","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"performance.md","filePath":"performance.md","lastUpdated":null}'),B={name:"performance.md"};function P(S,e,O,j,k,y){return r(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[t('<h1 id="Performance" tabindex="-1">Performance <a class="header-anchor" href="#Performance" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Performance {#Performance}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Each plot shows how fast PureBLAS is relative to a reference, against problem size. Higher is better and the dashed line is parity, so anything above it is a win. There is one curve per microarchitecture (Zen3 · AVX2, Zen4 · AVX-512, Zen5 · AVX-512) and one panel per operation, and the band around a curve is the q10–q90 spread of the pooled per-round ratios. Everything is single-threaded, Float64 and ComplexF64.</p><p>Roughly 87% of the measured cells are at or above <code>max(OpenBLAS, AOCL)</code>. The ones that are not are listed in <a href="./notes">Notes</a>, and most of them sit within a few percent.</p><p>For the numbers behind the plots, see <a href="https://github.com/el-oso/PureBLAS.jl/blob/master/bench/gen_table.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>bench/gen_table.md</code></a> against OpenBLAS and <a href="https://github.com/el-oso/PureBLAS.jl/blob/master/bench/gen_table_aocl.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>bench/gen_table_aocl.md</code></a> against AOCL. <a href="./methodology">Methodology</a> covers how the measurements are taken and how to read a cell; <a href="./notes">Notes</a> has the per-routine analysis and history.</p><h2 id="vs-OpenBLAS" tabindex="-1">vs OpenBLAS <a class="header-anchor" href="#vs-OpenBLAS" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;vs OpenBLAS {#vs-OpenBLAS}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+o+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+n+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+l+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+c+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+i+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+p+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+d+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+m+'" alt=""></p><h2 id="vs-AOCL" tabindex="-1">vs AOCL <a class="header-anchor" href="#vs-AOCL" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;vs AOCL {#vs-AOCL}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+h+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+_+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+g+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+u+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+v+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+f+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+A+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+b+'" alt=""></p><h2 id="vs-LinearAlgebra-generic-dual-numbers" tabindex="-1">vs LinearAlgebra generic (dual numbers) <a class="header-anchor" href="#vs-LinearAlgebra-generic-dual-numbers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;vs LinearAlgebra generic (dual numbers) {#vs-LinearAlgebra-generic-dual-numbers}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>One section, not two, because there is no second reference to compare against. <code>ForwardDiff.Dual{Tag,Float64,1}</code> is not a <code>BlasFloat</code>, so LinearAlgebra dispatches to its own generic fallback and no vendor BLAS is ever reached — OpenBLAS and AOCL have no dual-number path at all. The denominator here is therefore what a forward-mode AD user gets today <em>without</em> PureBLAS, and these ratios are <strong>not</strong> gate verdicts: the gate is <code>PB ≥ max(OpenBLAS, AOCL)</code>, which is undefined for this element type.</p><p><img src="'+L+'" alt=""></p><p>These numbers apply however you call PureBLAS. The native API (<code>PureBLAS.gemm!(…)</code>), the in-process reroute through <code>activate()</code>, and the <code>libpureblas.so</code> built for C hosts all run the same kernels.</p>',26)])])}const T=a(B,[["render",P]]);export{w as __pageData,T as default};
